AUDIO: 'So, Opus 5.5 has just been released , and Anthropic has published an official detailed guide on writing prompts for it. It seems like a lot of what worked in previous models now either costs more or slows down your work. So I'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'went through all their documentation and highlighted 12 tips that really matter, that will make it faster and cheaper to use this new model. So, let\'s begin. First, start with a medium effort level. " Effort" is'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a setting that determines how deeply Claude " thinks" before responding. In version 5.5, the recommended default value is now " medium". According to Anthropic testing, the medium effort level for Opus is the optimal balance'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of speed and quality of work. You can set this level as the default, work for a while, and if you feel the need for better quality, simply increase the effort level. Second, test your'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'effort levels. If you want the default effort level to be customized, you can ask Opus to test it. Because while the average level is recommended, it may vary for your specific'

[01:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'job . Therefore, the best way to determine your own default level is to test it on real tasks. A good way is to give Opus a prompt in which it chooses a real task from'

[01:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your daily practice, performs it at each effort level in version 5.5, and compares the results. And based on that, you can decide what level of effort is best to make typical. Third: you can now'

[01:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use the agents.md file in Claude Code. Claude Code now reads agents.md—the instruction file used by tools like Codex. If you update to the latest version and connect Claude to a folder where there is no'

[01:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Claude.md file, it will automatically look for agents.md and use it. So, if you are using multiple agents for programming, you can keep one agents.md as a single instruction file, instead of having to synchronize Claude.md'

[01:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and agents.md. By the way, I\'ve compiled all of these tips into a detailed PDF guide with all the prompts and resources you\'ll see in this video. You can simply send it directly to Claude and ask what tips are suitable for your setup and he will help you'

[02:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'implement them. The link to it is in the description below if you need it. Fourth: Change efforts without clearing the cache. So, when you communicate with your agent, it maintains what\'s called a cache— it\'s essentially short-term memory that allows you'

[02:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to save resources. Because before, when you changed effort settings during a conversation, Claude had to reread the entire conversation, the cache would be cleared, and that cost you tokens. With the release of Opus 5.5, this problem was solved. So even'

[02:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if you change your effort level in the middle of a conversation, the cache will be preserved and the agent won\'t have to reread the entire conversation again. Just note that Anthropic warns that this feature is in beta, so test it out on your settings'

[02:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'before fully using it. Fifth, use accumulated limit resets. Claude now accumulates usage resets for you . If you go into settings and then the usage section, you\'ll see a'

[02:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reset box, and with the launch of version 5.5, you probably got one for free. Best advice: save it for when your limits run out, then hit "reset" to regain access immediately. Just be sure to check the expiration date,'

[03:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because the bonus we received with release 5.5 expires on October 23. Sixth—Opus now marks queries that require showing a train of thought. If your requests or system instructions force'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Claude to describe a thought process before responding, this may be blocked with version 5.5. In fact, if you try this with a simple query, you will get an error stating "'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'extracting arguments " as the reason for the failure. It\'s clear that Anthropic is doing this to prevent other companies from copying the Opus 5.5 model. So if you previously had a legitimate reason to do so, unfortunately, this feature is now'

[03:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'unavailable in the new model class. Seventh— let him know that the previous answers are final. In long chats, Anthropic noticed that Opus 5.5 sometimes rephrases its early answers, which can be annoying with'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'short follow-up questions. If you notice this, it\'s a good idea to add a couple of sentences at the end of the Claude.md file indicating that past answers should be considered completed unless you ask for them again. Or, if you'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to activate this as needed, you can just create a simple skill that can be invoked at any time. Eighth—keep a checklist of tasks. According to Anthropic, in very long tasks, it has sometimes been observed that'

[04:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'version 5.5 will stop working even if the task is not yet technically 100% complete. This is because it now posts progress updates as it works, and sometimes one of those updates'

[04:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'completes a move, which can make it think the task is complete. If you see this, it\'s very easy to prevent it . Simply ask Opus to keep a checklist that it will check off as it works—you can'

[04:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'add this to your request. Then at the end of each turn he will check this list, which will increase the chances of fully completing the work assigned to him. Number nine—set a time limit. Opus 5.5 can keep a very close eye on'

[04:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how much time is spent on each task. So if you need something done within a certain amount of time, just set a time budget for Opus. You can tell it to complete a task in 3 minutes, and it will adjust its pace to'

[05:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'meet that limit . Number ten: If you don\'t have a time limit, just send him these two words. If you can\'t set a reasonable time limit, you can still speed up your agent\'s work. According to Anthropic, one of the best tips is'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to add the words “time is of the essence” to your query, and this will increase the speed of the task completion by the 5.5 model. During testing, this allowed agent teams to complete work earlier if time was'

[05:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'critical. Number eleven: Opus still has designer defaults. So when you ask Opus 5.5 to create a website without any design instructions, it will use the same default options. And Anthropic says that this was'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'foreseen by the developers. So, you get such screen backgrounds and italics in texts. And Opus\'s tips for making the look less generic simply replace one standard template with another. So the best advice is to give him a design system'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or even list the styles you want to avoid. Then the result will really look like your brand. I cover this in other videos on our platform, so watch them to get started. Number twelve— give it cropping and scaling tools. Opus'

[06:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '5.5 already recognizes diagrams and screenshots perfectly. But for very complex materials, like technical drawings, it\'s better to let it zoom in on the image . This not only allows the agent to focus on what\'s important, but also reduces'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'token usage because you\'re transmitting smaller fragments instead of the entire high- resolution image. So what you should do is give your agent access to tools that Anthropic itself recommends , like PIL and OpenCV—they\'re free Python libraries for'

[06:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cropping and scaling, so just send the highest resolution image you have , and it can crop the exact part it needs. So, that\'s all. As always, thanks for watching to the end, and'

